# Voice

**This project's own voice**, for anything drafted here that a person will
read. General writing quality is not this file's job and is not written
down here — the practice catalogue in force covers that, for every project
at once. What follows is only what is true of *this* project and would be
wrong somewhere else.

A section left `<undecided>` is not a gap to fill in with something
plausible. It means nobody has decided, and the general rules apply
unmodified.

## Voice Target

<One sentence. Who does this project sound like, in a way that is specific
to it? "A senior clinician writing to a referring colleague." "A brief to
a judge who has read the file." "The instruction card in the box." If the
honest answer is just "a sharp colleague typing quickly", leave this
undecided — that is the general default and does not need restating.>

## Audiences and Register

<Who reads what this project produces, and how formality differs between
them. A project with one audience needs one line. A project whose output
goes to both a regulator and a customer needs to say which is which, and
what changes.>

- **<audience>:** <register, and anything that shifts for them>

## Words This Project Uses, and Words It Avoids

<Domain terms only, and the reason. The right word for a thing here; a word
that means something specific in this field and must not be used loosely; a
name that must appear in a fixed form; a term the general rules would flag
but that is simply correct in this subject.

NOT a general banned-word list. If a word is bad writing everywhere, it is
already covered.>

| Word or phrase | Use, avoid, or fixed form | Why, in this project |
|---|---|---|
| <term> | <verdict> | <reason> |

## Overrides

<Anywhere this project deliberately departs from a rule in the catalogue,
with the reason. This is the section that earns the file: an override
nobody wrote down reads as a session ignoring a rule.

Each entry names the practice, states the departure, and says why the
general rule does not fit here. If there are none, say so — "none" is a
real answer and a useful one.>

- **<practice slug>:** <what this project does instead, and why>

## Examples

<Two or three short before-and-after pairs drawn from this project's own
material, if any are worth keeping. Generic examples are not — the
catalogue has those. A pair earns its place here only if it shows something
about this subject that a general rule would get wrong.>
